 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
-A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral. For uniform audits with refund-and-ban enforcement, free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and afterwards keeps targeting the newest identities. We report two registered tests; the second compares R3 with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
-We report four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3: \site{abs_q1_est}); treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}], so the ordering is not established beyond these markets, and the second attack family returned the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3's priority window changes the attacker's gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}. Third, the targeting also falls on honest newcomers: in a registered diagnostic at the headline market, R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of an honest replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, against \site{abs_hon_cs_1} under C$^*$. Fourth, under the prescribed D2+H3 searches, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
-All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references, six markets from one neighbourhood, fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
+A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral. For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement makes the ban costless, because replacement restores eligibility before the next allocation and preserves the payoff-relevant future states, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and afterwards keeps targeting the newest identities. We report two registered tests; the second compares R3 with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
+We report four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under the D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); treated as a sample of markets, the per-market D2+H3 differences give an exploratory $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}], so this analysis does not establish the ordering beyond these markets, and the second attack family returned the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3's priority window changes the attacker's gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}. Third, the targeting also falls on honest newcomers: in a registered diagnostic at the headline market, R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of an honest replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, against \site{abs_hon_cs_1} under C$^*$. Fourth, under the prescribed D2+H3 searches, R3 lowers the attacker's measured gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks lower the gross efficiency of all three core rules to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
+All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references on the evaluated markets (the six fresh markets from one neighbourhood for the comparisons, the headline market for the diagnostic and the welfare attacks), fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so population churn among honest providers is untested; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
 % END-EV2
 \end{abstract}
 
