22:All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references on the evaluated markets (the six fresh markets from one neighbourhood for the comparisons, the headline market for the diagnostic and the welfare attacks), fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so population churn among honest providers is untested; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
38:\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest provider's replacement identity well after its window, at a small cost to that provider: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
96:After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/2 \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits. In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest churn it would share the newest positions with honest newcomers.
132:\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.
191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. The D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in \site{r_x7_all} of \site{r_x7_all_n} market--rule cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a's differences are exactly zero. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1}, and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns slightly less per round and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
204:\caption{Registered honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (computed from unrounded report values, displayed rounded): an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50} while all others are honest. Audited share of the replacement's served jobs in its first three ten-round age bins; its profit per round and the refunds deducted from its receipts on false audit failures per ten-round bin, both divided by $S^*/N$ and averaged over the fifteen bins.}\label{tab:ph-honest}
291:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
331:\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity marks a provider that has replaced; population churn of honest identities is untested. With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power, and the registered honest-replacement diagnostic covers a single replacement only. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.

